


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: albrecht
@title: The role of <i>Affektenlehre</i> on musical characteristics of Chopin’s solo piano repertoire
@presenter: albrecht
@abstract: 

According to <i>Affektenlehre</i>, there is a relationship between
a work’s key and the affect expressed by that work,
typically reflected in semantic terms such as ‘pure’ or
‘gloomy.’ However, <i>Affektenlehre</i> extends far beyond
simple one-to-one relationships between tonality and
expression, and it is even misleading to call it a single
theory. In actuality, the theoretical literature of the
eighteenth and nineteenth centuries is peppered with
multiple different and competing ideas about the
relationship between key and expression. Despite the
popularity of theories of affect at the time, it remains
unclear to what extent <i>Affektenlehre</i> actually influenced
compositional practice in this era. Even if a composer
subscribed to an <i>Affektenlehre</i>, it remains an open question
as to which theory of affects a particular composer may
have adopted, and how consistent that composer was in
expressing the same affects with the same keys. This study
uses supervised machine learning to analyze the
relationship between key and automatically extracted
compositional features for the first published editions in
the Chopin Legacy in Open Access corpus, a symbolic corpus
of 182 Chopin piano works represented in kern format. There
is evidence that Chopin may have conceived of his music in
a way consistent with an <i>Affektenlehre</i>, and the pianos he
composed with evinced differences in intonation between
different keys. In this study, 676 musical features were
extracted from each piece and used to predict the pieces’
keys. Some keys were minimally represented, so features
were also used to predict the broader categories of major
vs. minor keys and flat-side vs. sharp-side vs.
no-accidental keys. The results showed the highest
classification accuracy for major vs. minor and then by
accidental category, but individual key classifications
were also significantly higher than chance. Implications
about affective expression in different keys or key
categories are drawn by examining the salient
distinguishing features, including melodic interval size,
semitone motion, ratio of parallel vs. contrary motion, and
the degree to which pitch-class distributions clumped
around a tonic-dominant polarity or spread out in more
harmonically adventurous ways. Finally, connections between
feature analysis and traditional theories of affect are
explored.


@@END: PRESENTATION



